// ------------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 1
const chapter1 = [
  h1("Chapter 1—Introduction"),
  h2("1.1 Background and Research Motivation"),
  body("Critical infrastructure increasingly operates as a cyber-physical system (CPS) in which sensing, computation, communication, and control interact continuously. Two of the most informative evidence streams an infrastructure operator can observe—low-altitude aircraft surveillance and network telemetry—are typically collected by different systems, stored with different time bases, and analyzed in different tools. An aircraft loitering at low altitude near a facility and a burst of anomalous network activity in the same minutes are therefore rarely examined together, and when they are, the comparison depends on manual alignment of timestamps that were never designed to agree. Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS-B) reports are unauthenticated and can be spoofed or jammed, so corroboration by an independent evidence stream is precisely what an operator would want; the absence of a common representation is what prevents it."),
  body("This praxis addresses that gap with an engineering artifact rather than a discovery claim. It builds a framework that (a) converts periodically sampled aircraft observations near a protected site into event-time-anchored point events, (b) aligns those events with network-flow windows under strict controls against information leakage, (c) fuses physical and cyber evidence in tabular, sequential, and relational models, and (d) explains the resulting prioritization with validated attribution. Because no public dataset pairs aircraft surveillance with coincident network telemetry, the alignment is a controlled replay of an independent NetFlow benchmark onto the aircraft timeline. That design choice is honest about its limits—the pairing is synthetic and non-causal—but it makes the framework testable, and the study's most consequential results come from testing it rigorously."),
  body("The research evolved through three validity corrections that are reported in full rather than hidden. The first replay reused hundreds of thousands of source flows across training, validation, and test partitions. The second removed that reuse but produced a held-out set in which the label was identical to the capture session. The third, regime-stratified design produced a held-out set with no session information, on which the benchmark's native label proved trivially separable. A graded target and a controlled coupling-injection study then gave the fusion question measurable content, and a final audit revealed that the relational model in all earlier comparisons had been trained for at most 50 gradient steps, far short of convergence. Each correction changed the conclusions; together they constitute the study's contribution: a self-auditing evaluation framework whose detection limits are stated rather than assumed."),
  h2("1.2 Research Context"),
  body("The physical data comprise twelve months (2025) of Flightradar24 (FR24) snapshots retrieved every five minutes for the airspace around Ted Stevens Anchorage International Airport (PANC), yielding 105,120 snapshots, 30,107 raw low-altitude observations, and 12,185 validated candidate point events from 2,035 distinct aircraft keys. The cyber data are the 2,365,424 flows of the NF-UNSW-NB15-v3 (NF3) NetFlow benchmark, which contains two capture sessions from January and February 2015 with nine attack categories. The unit of analysis is the individual point event, chosen after an episode audit showed that 12,158 of 12,168 contextual episodes contained a single observation, so an episode-level model would have had almost no within-episode structure to learn."),
  body("Two properties of the cyber benchmark, established in this study, shape everything that follows. At the scale of a ±120 s window the benchmark contains only three attack regimes: a 120-minute attack plateau that opens Session 1, 636 minutes of benign traffic after it, and a 714-minute attack plateau covering essentially all of Session 2. Consequently the native attack-window label is a regime label, separable by a single threshold on destination-host diversity, and any evaluation on it saturates. The graded attack-intensity target introduced in Chapter 3 varies within the plateaus and does not saturate."),
  h2("1.3 Problem Statement"),
  body("Aircraft surveillance and network telemetry near critical infrastructure are analyzed separately on misaligned time bases (Mitchell, 2026), so operators cannot corroborate unauthenticated, spoofable ADS-B reports (Riahi Manesh & Kaabouch, 2017)."),
  h2("1.4 Thesis Statement"),
  body("An event-time-aligned, leakage-audited fusion framework will show when combining aircraft observations with network telemetry improves prioritization of low-altitude events, and will state the limits of that evidence."),
  h2("1.5 Research Objectives"),
  ...numbered([
    "Construct a reproducible event-time-anchored representation of low-altitude aircraft point events and their recurrence context from periodically sampled surveillance.",
    "Align those events with network-flow windows under partition-disjoint, embargoed source blocks, and audit the alignment for source reuse, window overlap, class degeneracy, and session confounding.",
    "Quantify, with cluster-corrected uncertainty, whether physical, cyber, temporal, and relational evidence improves held-out prioritization relative to unimodal and sequential baselines, on both the benchmark's native label and a graded intensity label.",
    "Characterize, by controlled injection of known coupling and alignment error, the strength of cyber-physical coupling the framework can detect and the alignment fidelity it requires.",
    "Validate the explanation layer against ground truth by testing whether attribution tracks injected coupling, and release all artifacts with checksums and independent validation.",
  ]),
  h2("1.6 Research Questions"),
  ...numbered([
    "**RQ1.** How much does the choice of time basis—aircraft event time versus snapshot processing time—and alignment error more generally change the cyber target and the held-out ranking quality of cyber models?",
    "**RQ2.** Does a relation-aware graph model that reasons over flow neighbourhoods and event-to-event links outperform a sequential Transformer on the same inputs, on a target that is not saturated?",
    "**RQ3.** What is the value of relational structure and of lag-weighted relations within the graph model, and at what strength does injected cyber-physical coupling become detectable by fused models?",
  ]),
  h2("1.7 Research Hypotheses"),
  body("The hypotheses are stated in their revised, testable form; Section 3.12 records their original form and the reasons for revision. Margins are pre-registered and evaluated with the inference rules of Section 3.10."),
  ...numbered([
    "**H1′ (alignment fidelity).** Placing cyber windows on snapshot time rather than aircraft event time changes the attack-window label for at least 5% of events, and an alignment error of at least 120 s reduces the held-out AUPRC of the best cyber-only model by at least 0.02.",
    "**H2′ (relational versus sequential).** On the graded intensity target, a trained relational graph model exceeds the temporal Transformer by at least 0.03 AUPRC at coupling strengths κ = 0 and κ = 1.",
    "**H3′ (lag-weighted relations).** Lag- and relation-weighted message passing adds at least 0.05 AUPRC over an unweighted graph at κ ≥ 0.5 while keeping local inference p95 latency at or below 500 ms.",
    "**H4 (relational value; added).** The full graph model exceeds an otherwise identical event-only model with no inter-node relations (and therefore no flow-level evidence) by a positive margin whose ten-seed interval excludes zero.",
    "**H5 (detection floor; added).** Fusion of physical and cyber evidence improves held-out AUPRC over the best unimodal model, selected on validation, once the realized rank correlation between physical anomaly and cyber intensity reaches a stated threshold, and not below it; the gain over the cyber-only model is reported as the operational reference.",
  ]),
  h2("1.8 Research Scope and Limitations"),
  body("The scope is one site, one surveillance provider, one public NetFlow benchmark, and models that run on a single CPU. The aircraft and cyber records were not collected together; all pairings are controlled replays, and no result in this praxis is evidence of an actual cyber-physical incident near PANC. The benchmark supplies two capture sessions and three window-scale regimes, which limits held-out information to 27–49 non-overlapping 240 s source windows; every interval in this document is corrected for that clustering and every detection claim carries the corresponding floor. Coupling between the modalities is injected with known strength and is described as such. Explanations were validated for fidelity, stability, and provenance on the final trained checkpoints of three representative runs and, for the tabular models, by attribution tracking against injected coupling; one model–setting pair failed fidelity and is reported as such. No validation involved operators, which would require a human-subjects study outside the praxis boundary."),
  h2("1.9 Organization of the Praxis"),
  body("Chapter 2 reviews the literature on cyber-physical event representation, cross-domain fusion, ADS-B surveillance quality, explainable security analytics, temporal and relational modeling, and leakage-safe evaluation. Chapter 3 describes the data, the three-stage alignment design and its audits, the targets, the models and their training protocols, the coupling-injection study, the inference rules, and reproducibility controls. Chapter 4 reports descriptive statistics, alignment integrity results, ceiling-task results, the coupling study, the neural-model results including the training-budget correction and ten-seed inference, explainability validation, and latency. Chapter 5 evaluates each hypothesis, states conclusions and contributions, and recommends future work. Appendix A maps every method to its script and artifact."),
];

